        "k": "p",
        "t": "A **computer** is a machine that follows instructions to work with information. In a Liberian classroom that sentence has to do a lot of work, because the machine may be a **laptop** in the school office, a **phone** on a Duala market stall, a **radio** in the staff room, or — most days — no machine at all. Computing does not wait for a laboratory. When we give a classmate a finite list of steps, when we clap a pattern, when we sort pebbles into labelled cups, we are already computing: we are turning a job into **instructions** that a follower can run. This unit names the computers around us, including **human computers**, and it refuses the idea that Computer Science is a subject only a school with a lab can teach. A computer is a machine that follows instructions. Unplugged computing uses paper, cards and our bodies."
