        "k": "p",
        "t": "A **condition** is a yes-or-no test. If it is raining, we stay in; else we go out. Computers use the same idea: if the password is right, open the file; else show an error. **Selection** chooses a path. **Boolean** values are only true or false. Pupils who write 'always take the left path' in a maze that is blocked on Tuesdays need a condition: if the left path is blocked, take the right path. An algorithm without selection cannot handle a changing world. A condition is a yes-or-no test that chooses which steps to follow next."
